From 8c902e806b8f3ed1662db3aaf625a4ee938fdbe2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chen Xiaohui <graych@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 3 May 2026 22:04:27 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] vmm: memory: external uffd handler via in-process create
 + SCM_RIGHTS
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

When `--memory-zone uffd_socket=<path>` is set, register the zone for
userfaultfd-driven lazy population by an out-of-process handler.

Why ownership has to be inverted
================================
Linux's userfaultfd is bound to the mm of the syscall caller: a uffd
created in process P can only register VMAs that live in P's mm. A
parent process that opens a uffd and inherits it across fork/execve
to CH cannot register VMAs in CH's mm — UFFDIO_REGISTER returns
EINVAL because the VMA addresses are not in the uffd's mm.

The straightforward fix is to flip the ownership: CH (which owns the
chVA mmap of the memfd-backed zone) creates the uffd in-process,
registers MISSING on its own VMA, then hands the uffd fd back to the
external handler via SCM_RIGHTS. The kernel routes events to the
uffd_t, not to the current process, so the handler can drive
UFFDIO_COPY / UFFDIO_ZEROPAGE through the inherited fd.

Behaviour
=========
After successfully mmap'ing an existing_memory_file zone (commit 1),
create_ram_region runs the following sequence whenever uffd_socket
is set on the zone:

  1. SYS_userfaultfd(O_CLOEXEC | O_NONBLOCK) — creates uffd in CH mm
  2. UFFDIO_API negotiates features:
       MISSING_SHMEM | EVENT_REMOVE | EVENT_UNMAP | THREAD_ID
     EVENT_FORK is intentionally omitted — requesting it makes
     UFFDIO_API return EPERM unless CAP_SYS_PTRACE is held; the
     handler doesn't need cross-mm fork tracking. Bit values match
     <linux/userfaultfd.h>: 1<<5 / 1<<3 / 1<<6 / 1<<8 (an earlier
     draft had MISSING_SHMEM=1<<1 by mistake and tripped the EPERM
     check on EVENT_FORK).
  3. UFFDIO_REGISTER MISSING on [chVA, chVA+size) — addr is the
     post-mmap VA in CH's mm.
  4. connect() the uffd_socket UDS and sendmsg() one length-prefixed
     JSON message:
         {"type":"va_report","zone_id":"<id>","va_start":<u64>,"size":<u64>}
     with the uffd fd attached as SCM_RIGHTS ancillary.
  5. read() the handler's reply (`{"type":"ack"}` or
     `{"type":"error","msg":"..."}`). On error CH bubbles it up and
     refuses to start the VM.

This handshake completes synchronously inside create_ram_region, so
no vCPU runs before the handler is ready. The same path serves
cold-start (zone construction) and snapshot-restore (region
construction from the migration table); both are gated on
uffd_socket being Some.

Seccomp policy
==============
The vmm-thread allowlist gains:

  * SYS_userfaultfd                  (was unlisted)
  * UFFDIO_API ioctl    (0xc018_aa3f, _IOWR(0xAA, 0x3F, ...))
  * UFFDIO_REGISTER     (0xc020_aa00, _IOWR(0xAA, 0x00, ...))

Without these the vmm thread takes SIGSYS the moment the new path
fires. Filter changes apply to all configurations but are harmless
when uffd_socket is unset (CH never reaches the syscalls).

Backwards compat
================
No behaviour change unless uffd_socket is set on at least one zone.
file:// memory backing, hugepages, and the non-fd memfd_create path
all skip this code unconditionally.

Testing
=======
A handler stub at the UDS path that responds with
`{"type":"ack"}` on the first message is enough to satisfy the
handshake. Once the parent owns the uffd fd it drives
UFFDIO_COPY/ZEROPAGE per fault as usual.

Signed-off-by: Chen Xiaohui <graych@gmail.com>
---
 vmm/src/memory_manager.rs  | 242 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 vmm/src/seccomp_filters.rs |  17 +++
 2 files changed, 259 insertions(+)

